doc.para:
for statement
　The for statement is similar to the while statement, but it is more complex.
　The syntax is as follows:

for(clause 1; clause 2; clause 3)
	statement or block


　Here, in clause 1, you specify the expression you want to execute before entering the loop. Variable declarations can also be performed here, and variables declared here remain valid throughout the loop (they are deleted once the loop is exited). This clause can also be omitted.

　In clause 2, you specify a conditional expression. As long as this conditional expression is true, the statement or block is executed repeatedly. If omitted, it is always considered to be true.

　In clause 3, you specify the expression you want to execute at the end of the statement or block. This clause can also be omitted.

　The execution flow is as follows:
(1) First, execute clause 1
(2) Evaluate clause 2; if false, end without evaluating the statement or block
(3) Execute the statement or block
(4) Execute clause 3
(5) Evaluate clause 2; if false, exit the loop
(6) Repeat from (3)

　If a break statement is encountered inside the block, the loop is exited at that point. If a continue statement is encountered, it jumps to (4).


	var i,a=1;
	for(i=1;i<5;i++)
	{
		a*=i;
	}

	var i;
	for(i=0;i<10;i++)
	{
		if(obj[i]==0) continue; // i++ is executed even when continued
		if(obj[i]==1) break; // exits immediately on break
		func(obj[i]);
	}
